-- title:
--   Ideal generated by an mathcal O_D-homomorphism is stable
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime (a `Fact` instance for its primality being assumed), let $B$ be a commutative ring, and let $\Phi$ and $Y$ be formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over $B$ in the sense of `FormalODModule p B`: each consists of a commutative two-dimensional formal group law, an action $a \mapsto \mathrm{act}(a)$ of $\mathbb Z_{p^2} = W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ by pairs of power series, and a series $\varpi$, all law endomorphisms, with $\mathrm{act}(1)$ the identity, $\mathrm{act}$ multiplicative and additive for the law, $\varpi \circ \varpi = \mathrm{act}(p)$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ with $\sigma$ the Witt vector Frobenius. Let $\rho = (\rho_0,\rho_1)$ be a pair of power series in $B[\![x_0,x_1]\!]$ which is an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism from $\Phi$ to $Y$, i.e. the $\rho_j$ have zero constant coefficient and intertwine the two laws, and $\rho \circ \Phi.\mathrm{act}(a) = Y.\mathrm{act}(a) \circ \rho$ for all $a$ and $\rho \circ \Phi.\varpi = Y.\varpi \circ \rho$. Put $J = (\rho_0,\rho_1) \subseteq B[\![x_0,x_1]\!]$, the ideal spanned by the range of $\rho$. Then three things hold: for every $a \in \mathbb Z_{p^2}$ and every $G \in J$, the substitution of $\Phi.\mathrm{act}(a)$ into $G$ lies in $J$; for every $G \in J$ the substitution of $\Phi.\varpi$ into $G$ lies in $J$; and for every $G \in J$ the substitution of the law $\Phi.F$ into $G$ lies in the ideal of $B[\![x_0,x_1,y_0,y_1]\!]$ generated by the $\rho_j$ in the left-hand variables together with the $\rho_j$ in the right-hand variables.
--
--   This is the statement that the ideal cut out by a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules — the kernel in power-series form — is stable under the $\mathbb Z_{p^2}$-action and under $\varpi$, and is a subgroup for the formal group law. It is used in the kernel/quotient constructions for formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules, in particular by the statements about ideals generated by $\mathrm{act}(p^N)$ and by the construction of the moduli scheme whose points over nilpotent-thickening rings are described by such stability conditions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_subst_mem_span_of_isODHom.lean

